From 157d9f0a522b4295060647c3585ff63562d206b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Reed <reedswork@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:10:08 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] client: RSA keys of every size Chrome takes

Chrome 157 takes an RSA key of 1024 to 16384 bits, counted exactly,
anywhere in a verified chain and under any root. A key under 1024 bits
is weak (ERR_CERT_WEAK_KEY) and a key over 16384 bits does not parse in
BoringSSL. A root of the Chrome Root Store sets no higher floor.

ring verifies RSA signatures by keys of 2048 to 8192 bits only (rounded
up to whole bytes, so from 2041), so a chain or handshake signature by
a key of 1024 to 2040 or of 8193 to 16384 bits was refused.

The RSA PKCS#1 and PSS algorithms the certificate verifier uses are now
wrapped. A key in ring's range still goes to ring; RustCrypto rsa
verifies the others, with the same hashes and paddings (PSS salt as long
as the hash). Every key is held to BoringSSL's rsa_check_public_key (n
and e odd, e of 2 to 33 bits) and to Chrome's floor and ceiling. RSA key
exchange takes the same sizes; it took 1024 to 8192 bits.

Adds sha2 =0.10.9 with its oid feature. It was already in the lock, for
other crates.
